%N A398302 In the canonical representation of n as a product of powers of prime numbers, a(n) is the position of the last prime with an exponent greater than 1, or 0 if n is squarefree.
%C A398302 In canonical representation, the primes appear in increasing order. For n, their respective exponents are listed in row n of A124010.
%C A398302 Each number k first occurs at a(A286630(k)).
%H A398302 Michael De Vlieger, <a href="/A398302/b398302.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A398302 180 = 2^2 * 3^2 * 5^1. The last exponent greater than 1 belongs to the 2nd prime power in the product, so a(180) = 2.
%e A398302 42 = 2^1 * 3^1 * 7^1 is squarefree, so a(42) = 0.
%t A398302 f[x_] := Length[#] - LengthWhile[Reverse[#], # == 1 &] &@ FactorInteger[x][[All, -1]]; Array[f, 120]
%o A398302 (Python)
%o A398302 from sympy import factorint
%o A398302 def a(n):
%o A398302     f = factorint(n)
%o A398302     return len(f) - next((k for k, p in enumerate(sorted(f, reverse=True)) if f[p] > 1), len(f))
%o A398302 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 101)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Aug 05 2026
